{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The argument rests on the premise that two critics producing the same failure verdict on one artifact 'implies' a shared structural assumption or degenerate test — but it explicitly acknowledges the alternative (a) that the critics share a common failure mode, which is precisely what §11.3 is designed to flag. By the target's own logic, the repeated failure is equally consistent with both critics genuinely sharing a deficiency, so the conclusion that the flag is 'actually evidence of a test-design deficiency' does not follow; it merely selects the explanation favorable to the critics without ruling out the very critic-deficiency explanation the ritual is meant to detect.","premise":"The argument concedes that both critics share a common failure mode as a live explanation (option a), yet concludes the flag reflects a test-design deficiency rather than a critic deficiency.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"0c61961a8085","quote":"both were found to have failed, which implies either (a) the critics share a common failure mode or (b) the test case itself is degenerate"}],"counterexample":null}]}